This must never be used as a real authentication credential.

## 5.2 JWT expiration must be exactly 24 hours

Set and enforce the backend JWT expiration to 24 hours.

Target configuration:

`JWT_EXPIRATION=86400000`

This is milliseconds.

Inspect `JwtTokenUtil.java` and all related configuration before modifying it.

Ensure that:

- The signing timestamp is generated at login.
- The expiration timestamp is exactly 24 hours after issuance.
- The JWT signature is validated.
- The expiration claim is validated.
- Expired JWTs are rejected.
- Invalid JWTs are rejected.
- Tampered JWTs are rejected.
- Missing JWTs are rejected for protected endpoints.

Do not rely on a frontend countdown to enforce expiration.

The backend must independently reject expired JWTs with HTTP 401.

## 5.3 JWT storage

Use `sessionStorage` for the browser token if compatible with the existing authentication design.

Do not store JWTs in localStorage.

Do not store passwords in browser storage.

Do not store authentication credentials in URL parameters.

The frontend must:

- Save the backend-issued JWT only after successful login.
- Normalize the Bearer prefix so it is never duplicated.
- Automatically attach the token to protected API requests.
- Remove the token on logout.
- Remove invalid or expired tokens after a 401 response.
- Redirect to the login screen after session expiration.

Do not silently renew an expired JWT.

Do not invent refresh-token behavior unless the backend already implements a properly secured refresh-token architecture.

